      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by differentiating the given function: the derivative of log[(s+1)/(s-1)] is F'(s) = 1/(s+1) - 1/(s-1) = -2/(s^2 - 1). The Laplace transform property states that if ℒ{f(t)} = F(s), then ℒ{t f(t)} = -F'(s), so ℒ{t f(t)} = 2/(s^2 - 1). The inverse Laplace transform of 2/(s^2 - 1) is 2 sinh(t), since ℒ{sinh(t)} = 1/(s^2 - 1). Therefore, t f(t) = 2 sinh(t), yielding f(t) = (2 sinh t)/t, which matches option D.",
