    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nThe minimum required \\(wl \\geq 1.818\\) in² for both shear and bearing stresses to be ≤11,000 psi and ≤22,000 psi, respectively. Option B (\\(w = t = 0.75\\) in., \\(l = 2.5\\) in.) gives \\(wl = 1.875\\) in², satisfying both with stresses ≈10,667 psi. Options A and D fail shear or bearing; C works but uses a larger-than-standard key size for a 3 in. shaft.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by deriving the key design formulas from the torque and stresses. The tangential force is calculated as \\(F = 2T / d = 2 \\times 30,000 / 3 = 20,000\\) lbs. For shear, \\(\\tau = 2T / (d \\cdot w \\cdot l) \\leq 11,000\\) psi yields \\(w \\cdot l \\geq 20,000 / 11,000 \\approx 1.818\\) in². For bearing, \\(\\sigma_b = 4T / (d \\cdot l \\cdot t) \\leq 22,000\\) psi, and for a square key (\\(t = w\\)), also yields \\(w \\cdot l \\geq 1.818\\) in².\n\nEach option is then evaluated: A (\\(w = 0.5\\), \\(l = 3\\)) gives \\(w \\cdot l = 1.5 < 1.818\\), failing both stresses; B (\\(w = t = 0.75\\), \\(l = 2.5\\)) gives \\(1.875 > 1.818\\), satisfying both at ≈10,667 psi; C (\\(w = t = 1\\), \\(l = 2\\)) gives \\(2 > 1.818\\), also satisfying but oversized; D (\\(w = 0.75\\), \\(t = 0.5\\), \\(l = 2.5\\)) passes shear (\\(w \\cdot l = 1.875\\)) but fails bearing (\\(\\sigma_b = 32,000 > 22,000\\) psi).\n\nOption B is selected as the most likely correct answer, as it meets the minimum requirements precisely, aligns with standard square key sizing for a 3 in. shaft (typically 3/4 in.), and avoids overdesign seen in C.",
