      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nThe electric displacement field \\( \\mathbf{D} = \\left[ r + \\frac{1}{r^2} \\right] U(r_0 - r) \\hat{r} \\) (zero outside) yields \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = 3 \\) C/m³ uniform volume charge density for \\( r < r_0 \\). Near \\( r = 0 \\), \\( 4\\pi r^2 D_r \\to 4\\pi \\) C, indicating a point charge of \\( 4\\pi \\) C at the origin. Gauss's law for \\( r > r_0 \\) requires total enclosed charge zero, so surface charge density \\( \\sigma = -\\left[ r_0 + \\frac{1}{r_0^2} \\right] \\) C/m² on \\( r = r_0 \\), matching option J.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by applying Gauss's law for D, \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = \\rho \\), in spherical coordinates for the given \\( \\mathbf{D} = \\left[ r + \\frac{1}{r^2} \\right] U(r_0 - r) \\hat{r} \\). Inside \\( r < r_0 \\), compute \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = \\frac{1}{r^2} \\frac{d}{dr} (r^2 D_r) \\), where \\( r^2 D_r = r^3 + 1 \\), so \\( \\frac{d}{dr} (r^3 + 1) = 3r^2 \\), yielding \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = 3 \\) C/m³ uniform charge density. Near \\( r = 0 \\), \\( D_r \\approx \\frac{1}{r^2} \\), so the flux through a small Gaussian sphere gives \\( 4\\pi r^2 D_r \\to 4\\pi \\) C, confirming a point charge of \\( 4\\pi \\) C at the origin. For \\( r > r_0 \\), \\( \\mathbf{D} = 0 \\), so total enclosed charge is zero; the interior charge \\( Q_\\text{inside} = 4\\pi r_0^2 D_r(r_0^-) = 4\\pi r_0^3 + 4\\pi \\) C requires surface charge \\( \\sigma = -\\frac{Q_\\text{inside}}{4\\pi r_0^2} = -\\left[ r_0 + \\frac{1}{r_0^2} \\right] \\) C/m² on \\( r = r_0 \\). These values—uniform density of 3 C/m³, surface charge \\( -\\left[ r_0 + \\frac{1}{r_0^2} \\right] \\), and point charge of \\( 4\\pi \\) C—exactly match option J.",
