    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nSchamroth's sign (obliteration of the diamond-shaped window when opposing distal phalanges) is the most reliable bedside test for clubbing due to its objectivity and high sensitivity/specificity. Inspecting the nail-bed angle from the side (A) is also standard but more subjective. Other options assess secondary features or are unreliable.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe thought process begins by identifying clubbing as a clinical sign involving bulbous distal finger enlargement, commonly assessed via specific physical exam maneuvers. Standard methods include evaluating Lovibond's angle (nail-nail bed angle >180 degrees, best seen from the side as in option A) and Schamroth's sign (option E), which tests for obliteration of the diamond-shaped window when opposing distal phalanges. Among options, Schamroth's sign is selected as most reliable due to its objective nature, high sensitivity, and specificity as a confirmatory bedside test, outperforming subjective assessments like angle inspection or secondary features (e.g., curvature in B, fluctuancy in J). Other options (C–D, F–I) are dismissed as they do not align with established diagnostic criteria.",
