    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nRising incomes and urbanization drive a dietary shift toward higher-value animal proteins (Bennett's Law), with global data from FAO and World Bank showing sharp increases in meat and dairy consumption in emerging economies. Staples like grains decline proportionally, while meat/dairy rise to meet demand for convenient, nutritious foods. Other options like sugars or oils increase secondarily but not as dominantly.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying key economic principles: rising incomes and urbanization lead to dietary shifts, as described by Bennett's Law, where consumption moves from staple carbohydrates toward higher-value animal proteins. Global trends from sources like FAO and World Bank reports confirm sharp increases in meat and dairy demand in developing regions, driven by affordability and convenience in urban settings. Among the options, A (Meat and Dairy products) aligns most directly with this evidence, as staples (e.g., B, G, J) decline proportionally, while secondary rises in items like sugars (H) or oils (D) are less prominent.",
