    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "G\n\nComplete bilateral cleft palate results from failure of the two palatine processes (lateral palatine shelves derived from the maxillary prominences) to elevate, meet, and fuse in the midline. This prevents formation of the secondary palate. Other options include extraneous processes unrelated to palate fusion.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying that complete bilateral cleft palate involves a full cleft of the secondary palate on both sides, resulting from embryologic failure of midline fusion. Embryologically, the secondary palate forms from the two palatine processes (lateral palatine shelves outgrowths of the maxillary processes), which must elevate and fuse centrally; their non-fusion directly causes this defect. Options including additional processes like frontonasal, maxillary, secondary nasal, or mandibular refer to structures involved in primary palate, lip, or nasal development, making G (\"palatine processes\") the most precise and standard match based on core embryology texts.",
