    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "CTL exhaustion (option F) is the primary reason vigorous CD8 T cell responses fail in chronic HIV infection, as prolonged antigen exposure leads to dysfunctional, exhausted CTLs despite initial robust expansion. Epitope escape (H) contributes but does not negate the vigorous response against recognized epitopes. Other options like suboptimal CTL (B) contradict the \"vigorous\" description.\n\n\\boxed{F}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying CD8 cells as cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs), which expand vigorously in chronic HIV infection but fail to clear the virus. Among the options, CTL exhaustion (F) is selected as the most direct explanation, as chronic antigen exposure causes these cells to become dysfunctional despite high numbers and initial activity, a hallmark of HIV persistence. Other options are eliminated: epitope escape (H) occurs but does not fully account for the vigorous response against existing epitopes; inadequate MHC presentation (A) or suboptimal CTL (B) contradict the vigorous response; and irrelevant factors like interferon gamma overproduction (C) or B cell issues (J) do not apply. This aligns with established virology, confirming F as the most likely correct answer.",
