    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe patient's symptoms of anxiety, hyperactivity, and insomnia occur specifically 3-4 days before menses, resolving into depression, anorexia, irritability, and lethargy at menses onset, which is characteristic of premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD). Other options like bipolar disorders or major depression lack this strict cyclic, perimenstrual pattern and do not align with her normal exam and absent psychiatric history. Infertility history may add stress but does not explain the timing.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe symptoms described—anxiety, hyperactivity, and insomnia 3-4 days premenstrually, followed by acute depression, anorexia, irritability, and lethargy at menses onset—directly correlate with the luteal phase and early follicular phase of the menstrual cycle, defining premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD, option C). This pattern is recurrent, tied to menses, and absent of prior psychiatric history, distinguishing it from chronic or spontaneous conditions like generalized anxiety disorder (A), bipolar disorders (B, H), persistent depressive disorder (E), or major depressive disorder (J). Options such as cyclothymic personality (D), PTSD (F), seasonal affective disorder (G), and adjustment disorder (I) are ruled out due to lack of fitting triggers, persistence, seasonality, or trauma history, with infertility noted as unrelated to the cyclic timing.",
