    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C. Geological**\n\nGeological disasters like earthquakes have caused the highest death tolls in single events (e.g., 1556 Shaanxi earthquake: ~830,000 deaths) and cumulatively rank highest per disaster databases like EM-DAT. Hydrological events (floods) and meteorological (cyclones/droughts) follow closely but with slightly lower peaks. Fires cause far fewer deaths overall.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the question, which seeks the natural disaster category responsible for the maximum historical deaths, using standard classifications from disaster databases like EM-DAT. Options are evaluated as follows: Hydrological (e.g., floods) has high tolls like the 1931 China floods (1-4 million deaths); Meteorological (e.g., cyclones, droughts) includes events like the 1876-1878 drought (millions); Geological (e.g., earthquakes) features the highest single-event deaths, such as the 1556 Shaanxi earthquake (~830,000); Fires (wildfires) consistently rank lowest. Geological is selected due to its top cumulative and peak impacts in verified records, surpassing other categories despite close competition from floods.",
