      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nIn non-diabetic high-risk pregnancies, non-stress test (NST) monitoring is typically performed twice weekly, corresponding to an interval of approximately 72 hours. This contrasts with diabetic high-risk pregnancies, where daily NST (every 24 hours) is often recommended. The 72-hour interval (option B) is the most likely standard based on common obstetric protocols.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer identifies B. 72 hrs as the correct choice for NST monitoring in non-diabetic high-risk pregnancies. Standard obstetric guidelines, such as those from ACOG, recommend twice-weekly NST for most high-risk conditions without diabetes, which aligns with an interval of approximately 72 hours (every 3 days). This frequency is less intensive than daily monitoring (24 hrs, option D), typically reserved for diabetic pregnancies, while 48 hrs and 96 hrs do not match the common twice-weekly standard as precisely.",
