    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nLight transmitting wedges, typically made of optical glass, achieve high transmission due to minimal absorption and reflection losses (around 4% per uncoated surface, yielding ~92% overall for visible light). This aligns with the 90-95% range in standard optics specifications. Lower ranges (A-C) are less typical for dedicated transmitting optics.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying light transmitting wedges as optical components, such as glass wedges used for beam deviation, which are designed for high light transmission. It calculates typical losses: approximately 4% reflection per air-glass surface (Fresnel reflection), resulting in about 8% total loss for two surfaces and negligible absorption in high-quality glass, yielding around 92% transmission for visible light. Among the options, 90-95% (D) best matches this calculated value, as lower ranges (A-C) imply higher losses inconsistent with standard uncoated optical wedges.",
