    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Anchorage not being applied here:\n\nOptions: \nA. Reciprocal\nB. Stationary\nC. Intramaxillary\nD. Intraoral\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nReciprocal anchorage involves equal and opposite tooth movements where units provide mutual support, effectively not requiring additional stationary or reinforced anchorage to be \"applied\" in the traditional sense. Stationary anchorage relies on preventing movement of the anchorage unit, while intramaxillary and intraoral refer to locations where anchorage is actively utilized. Thus, reciprocal best fits \"anchorage not being applied here.\"\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by recognizing the question as related to orthodontic anchorage classifications, where the phrase \"Anchorage not being applied here\" indicates a scenario lacking traditional anchorage control. Options are evaluated as follows: reciprocal anchorage (A) involves mutual movement of units toward each other, balancing forces without needing a stationary unit or external reinforcement; stationary anchorage (B) requires the anchorage unit to remain unmoved, directly applying anchorage; intramaxillary (C) and intraoral (D) describe spatial categories where anchorage is actively sourced from teeth within the same jaw or mouth. Reciprocal anchorage is identified as the most likely fit, as it does not rely on preventing movement in an anchorage unit, aligning with the absence of applied anchorage.",
