    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nScleroderma (systemic sclerosis) is associated with the worst pregnancy outcomes among these options due to high risks of renal crisis, accelerated hypertension, pulmonary hypertension, and maternal mortality, often contraindicating pregnancy. In contrast, SLE nephritis has improved outcomes with careful management, while IgA nephropathy and ADPKD generally tolerate pregnancy well if kidney function is stable. Evidence from obstetric nephrology guidelines supports scleroderma's highest maternal and fetal risks.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the question, which identifies the renal disorder linked to the worst pregnancy outcomes among the given options. Each condition is evaluated based on known medical literature: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) carries risks like disease flares and preeclampsia but has manageable outcomes with monitoring; IgA nephropathy typically yields favorable results if kidney function is stable; autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is generally well-tolerated with blood pressure control. Scleroderma stands out due to its association with severe complications, including renal crisis, accelerated hypertension, pulmonary hypertension, and high maternal mortality rates, as supported by obstetric nephrology guidelines. Thus, scleroderma is selected as the condition with the poorest pregnancy prognosis.",
