    "global index": "MedMCQA_1523",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: NK cells are effective against viral infected cells only if the cell with infection:\n\nOptions: \nA. Express MHC class I proteins\nB. Unable to express MHC class I proteins\nC. Express MHC class II proteins\nD. Unable to express MHC class II proteins\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nNK cells recognize and kill virally infected cells primarily through the \"missing self\" mechanism, where the absence of MHC class I proteins (often downregulated by viruses to evade T cells) removes inhibitory signals, allowing NK activation. Cells expressing MHC class I are protected, while MHC class II expression is irrelevant to NK function.  \n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying NK cells' primary mechanism: they target cells lacking MHC class I proteins via the \"missing self\" hypothesis, as viruses downregulate MHC class I to evade cytotoxic T cells, thereby making infected cells vulnerable. Evaluating options: A is incorrect because MHC class I expression inhibits NK cells; B is correct as the absence of MHC class I enables NK killing; C and D are irrelevant since MHC class II relates to CD4 T cell activation, not NK recognition. Thus, option B aligns directly with the immunological principle that NK cells are effective against virally infected cells unable to express MHC class I.",
