    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nDuring exercise within physiological limits, sympathetic stimulation enhances myocardial contractility, allowing the ventricle to eject more blood and thus decreasing end-systolic volume (ESV). Increased venous return also contributes to greater stroke volume without proportionally increasing ESV. Option D may apply to supramaximal efforts beyond physiological limits.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by defining end-systolic volume (ESV) as the amount of blood remaining in the ventricle after systolic contraction. During exercise within physiological limits, sympathetic nervous system activation increases myocardial contractility and venous return (preload), enabling the heart to eject a greater fraction of blood and thereby reducing ESV. This aligns with option A, as standard physiological responses to moderate exercise—such as elevated heart rate and stroke volume—do not lead to ESV increases or biphasic changes, ruling out options B, C, and D.",
