    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nThe most common cause of interdental papilla protruding from the rubber dam is punch holes placed too close together, causing the dam to ride up on the papillae when stretched over normally spaced teeth. Holes too far apart (C) would create slack rubber that covers the papilla. Inflammation (A) or light-weight dam (B) are less common causes.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the problem: interdental papilla protruding from the rubber dam during dental procedures, which occurs due to improper hole placement on the dam. It evaluates the options based on rubber dam mechanics—holes punched too close together (option D) result in insufficient space between them, causing the dam to stretch tightly and allow papillae to push through when seated on teeth with normal spacing. In contrast, holes too far apart (C) create excess rubber that covers the papilla; inflammation (A) may contribute but is not the primary cause; and light-weight dam (B) affects durability rather than positioning. Thus, option D is selected as the most common cause through this logical elimination.",
