    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nA flexible major connector in a removable partial denture (RPD) fails to provide the necessary rigidity, allowing uneven stress distribution and excessive movement that torques abutment teeth, leading to periodontal ligament (PDL) destruction. Rigid connectors are essential to unify the prosthesis and protect supporting structures. Other options like ill-fitting or occlusion issues are less directly tied to flexibility.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by recognizing the question pertains to removable partial dentures (RPDs) in dentistry, where major connectors must be rigid to distribute occlusal forces evenly across abutment teeth and mucosa. A flexible major connector disrupts this by permitting differential movement between RPD components, generating uneven torquing forces on abutment teeth. This directly aligns with option B, as such forces cause periodontal ligament (PDL) destruction over time. Options A, C, and D are less accurate: A contradicts the reduced effectiveness from flexibility; C relates more to initial fabrication errors than functional flexibility; and D stems indirectly from movement but not as the primary consequence.",
