      "generated_answer": "Stress breakers are avoided in partial denture construction primarily due to their harmful effects on abutment teeth, as they permit independent movement of the distal extension saddle, leading to torquing and overload forces. Other options like expense, vertical dimension changes, or fabrication difficulty are not the main reasons.  \n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying that stress breakers in partial dentures aim to redirect stress from abutment teeth to supporting mucosa but are not used due to their unintended consequences. Among the options, D is selected because stress breakers allow independent movement of distal extensions, causing torquing forces and overload on abutments, which outweighs their benefits as per standard RPD design principles. Options A, B, and C are dismissed as secondary issues, with no evidence that expense, vertical dimension increase, or fabrication difficulty are primary factors for avoidance.",
