    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nHedstrom files (likely \"Headstrom\") have thin, sharp flutes that make them efficient cutters but highly brittle and prone to fracture under torsion or fatigue compared to others. Triangular reamers and rhomboid/square files have more robust cross-sections with greater material distribution for the same size 55 diameter and steel.  \n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the context as endodontic instruments, all made of the same steel and size 55, so differences in fracture proneness stem from cross-sectional designs affecting stress distribution and material thickness. Cross-sections are compared: triangular reamers have three cutting edges with moderate flexibility; rhomboid (K-file) and square files offer greater material area and stiffness for better torsional resistance; Hedstrom files feature thin, sharp, spiral flutes that concentrate stress, making them the most brittle. Endodontic literature confirms Hedstrom files fracture most readily under torsion or fatigue due to their design, leading to the selection of option D as the most prone to fracture.",
