      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nIn porcelain-fused-to-metal (PFM) restorations, repeated porcelain fractures are primarily caused by flexure or distortion of an inadequately designed metal framework (e.g., insufficient thickness or poor support) under occlusal loads, as porcelain lacks ductility. While occlusal trauma (A) can contribute, it is secondary to design flaws. Firing issues (C) or poor metal conditioning (D) more often cause initial bond failure rather than repeated mechanical fractures.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying the question's focus: repeated fractures in porcelain-fused-to-metal (PFM) restorations, which typically result from mechanical stresses rather than initial fabrication errors. Each option is evaluated as follows: A (Occlusal trauma) can contribute to fractures but is not primary for repetition, as it often acts secondarily; B (Inadequately designed framework) is the leading cause, due to flexure from thin or unsupported metal under load, as porcelain cannot tolerate distortion; C (Improper firing schedule) and D (Failure to use metal conditioner) primarily lead to poor bonding or initial defects, not ongoing mechanical failures. Prosthodontic principles confirm that inadequate framework design, such as insufficient thickness in high-stress areas, directly causes repeated porcelain fractures by allowing movement. Thus, B is selected as the most likely correct answer.",
