      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nDental caries (cavities) are primarily caused by bacteria metabolizing frequent sucrose intake, leading to acid production that demineralizes enamel, a key factor in teenagers' high snacking habits. While poor hygiene (D) enables plaque retention and infrequent dental visits (C) worsen outcomes, the frequency of sugar exposure is the most direct initiator. Rapid growth (A) has minimal direct relation.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying dental caries as cavities caused by bacteria fermenting sugars like sucrose into acids that erode tooth enamel. Among the options, frequency of sucrose intake (B) is evaluated as the most direct cause, as it aligns with teenagers' common habits of frequent snacking on sugary foods, which prolongs acid exposure per the Stephan curve. Other options—rapid growth (A), negligence in dental visits (C), and carelessness in hygiene (D)—are secondary factors that exacerbate but do not initiate the process as directly as sugar frequency.",
