    "global index": "MedMCQA_1186",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following change can be done in insulin structure so that there is least change in the function of insulin:\n\nOptions: \nA. Interchange of A1 & A4\nB. Interchange of B29 and B30\nC. Interchange of A5 & A6\nD. Breaking disulphide linkages\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nInterchanging B29 (lysine) and B30 (threonine) at the C-terminus of the B chain minimally affects insulin's receptor binding and activity, as these residues are primarily involved in dimerization rather than core function. In contrast, swapping A5 (glutamine) and A6 (cysteine) disrupts the critical A6-A11 disulfide bond, A1-A4 alters N-terminal flexibility essential for structure, and breaking disulfide linkages collapses the overall fold. Studies on insulin analogs confirm B29-B30 swap retains nearly full potency.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning evaluates each option's impact on insulin's structure and function, prioritizing minimal disruption to receptor binding and overall activity. Breaking disulfide linkages (D) severely destabilizes the folded structure, eliminating viability. Swapping A5 (glutamine) and A6 (cysteine) (C) relocates a critical cysteine needed for the A6-A11 intra-chain disulfide bond, causing structural collapse. Interchanging A1 (glycine) and A4 (glutamic acid) (A) disrupts N-terminal flexibility vital for proper conformation and processing. In contrast, swapping B29 (lysine) and B30 (threonine) (B) involves C-terminal residues mainly linked to dimerization, not core receptor interactions, as confirmed by insulin analog studies showing retained potency. Thus, option B results in the least functional change.",
