    "global index": "MedMCQA_557",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A G2 P1+0+0 diabetic mother present at 32 weeks pregnancy, there is history of full term fetal demise in last pregnancy. Her vitals are stable, sugar is controlled and fetus is stable. Which among the following will be the most appropriate management?\n\nOptions: \nA. To induce at 38 weeks\nB. To induce at 40 weeks\nC. Cesarean section at 38 weeks\nD. To wait for spontaneous delivery\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nFor a diabetic mother with a history of term fetal demise, guidelines recommend delivery by 38-39 weeks to mitigate stillbirth risk, despite good control. Induction is preferred over cesarean without obstetric indications, given her prior term delivery. Waiting or delaying to 40 weeks increases perinatal risks.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying key risk factors: pregestational diabetes and a history of full-term fetal demise, which elevate the risk of recurrent stillbirth despite stable maternal vitals, controlled glycemia, and fetal well-being at 32 weeks. Standard guidelines, such as those from ACOG, recommend delivery for pregestational diabetes between 39 weeks with good control, but earlier (37-39 weeks) for additional risks like prior stillbirth, making options B (induce at 40 weeks) and D (wait for spontaneous delivery) inappropriate due to heightened perinatal hazards. Among A (induce at 38 weeks) and C (cesarean at 38 weeks), induction is selected as the most appropriate since the patient is G2 P1 (indicating a prior term delivery, likely vaginal), with no contraindications to labor, thus avoiding unnecessary cesarean risks.",
