      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nThe study found that ~60% of participants (a majority of the 7 cases) achieved average-range word and paragraph reading after left hemispherectomy, with spared phonological processing strongly correlating to reading levels, similar to typical readers. This demonstrates that the isolated right hemisphere can support literacy. Later seizure onset further predicted better outcomes, reinforcing the affirmative finding.\n\n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by analyzing the question, which asks if the isolated right hemisphere can read following left cerebral hemispherectomy, based on the provided context. The context details a study of seven participants where approximately 60% achieved average word and paragraph reading levels, with phonological processing—key for reading—spared in the majority and correlating strongly with reading performance, akin to typical readers. This evidence directly supports option A (\"yes\") as the most likely correct answer, as it shows functional reading ability mediated by the right hemisphere, rather than outright failure (B, \"no\") or mere uncertainty (C, \"maybe\").",
