    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: [\"In this study, an expert panel assessed causality of adverse reports by using the WHO global introspection (GI) method. The same reports were independently assessed using 15 published algorithms. The causality assessment level 'possible' was considered the lower limit for a report to be considered to be drug related. For a given algorithm, sensitivity was determined by the proportion of reports simultaneously classified as drug related by the algorithm and the GI method. Specificity was measured as the proportion of reports simultaneously considered non-drug related. The analysis was performed for the total sample and within serious or unexpected events.\", 'Five hundred adverse reports were studied. Algorithms presented high rates of sensitivity (average of 93%, positive predictive value of 89%) and low rates of specificity (average of 7%, negative predictive value of 31%).']\n\nQuestion: Can decisional algorithms replace global introspection in the individual causality assessment of spontaneously reported ADRs?\n\nOptions: \nA. yes\nB. no\nC. maybe\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**B**\n\nAlgorithms showed high sensitivity (93%) but very low specificity (7%), meaning they correctly identify most drug-related reports but falsely classify many non-drug-related ones as drug-related. This imbalance indicates they cannot reliably replace global introspection, which serves as the reference standard.  \n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by examining the study's key metrics: algorithms achieved high sensitivity (93% average), indicating they correctly identified most reports deemed drug-related by the WHO global introspection (GI) method, but exhibited low specificity (7% average), meaning they incorrectly classified many non-drug-related reports as drug-related.  \nThis discrepancy highlights a critical limitation, as reliable replacement of GI requires both high sensitivity and high specificity for accurate individual causality assessments.  \nThus, the poor specificity leads directly to the conclusion that decisional algorithms cannot replace global introspection, selecting option B as the most appropriate answer.",
