    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_196",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the Neo-Riemannian theory, which chord does the C major triad change into after applying the R, L, P transformations (N relation - Nebenverwandt)?\n\nOptions: \nA. a minor triad\nB. ♭B major triad\nC. f minor triad\nD. ♯c minor triad\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In Neo-Riemannian theory, the N (Nebenverwandt) transformation relates the C major triad (C-E-G) to the F minor triad (F-A♭-C) via voice leading with one common tone (C) and two semitone motions in the same direction (E→F, G→A♭).  \nThis distinguishes it from R (to A minor), L (to E minor), and P (to C minor).  \nThus, the correct choice is C.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by identifying the N (Nebenverwandt) transformation in Neo-Riemannian theory, which relates major and minor triads through specific voice leading: one common tone and two semitone motions in the same direction. For the C major triad (C-E-G), applying N preserves C as the common tone while moving E up a semitone to F and G up a semitone to A♭, resulting in the F minor triad (F-A♭-C). This matches option C, distinguishing N from other transformations: R produces A minor (two common tones, G→A up semitone), L produces E minor (common E and G, C→B down semitone), and P produces C minor (common C and G, E→E♭ down semitone).",
