      "generated_answer": "①, ②, ③, and ④ can be played via standard pedal harp glissando (diatonic/modes with consistent accidentals; dom7 and dim7/half-dim7 chord glissandi via enharmonic pedal settings repeating chord tones), but not ⑤ (chromatic requires non-standard multi-hand/special technique).  \nThus, option B.\n\n\\boxed{B}\n\n## Explanation\n\nPedal harp glissandi produce diatonic sequences by setting pedals to assign consistent accidentals to each pitch class, enabling scales or modes with seven distinct pitch classes.  \n① C diatonic scale (C major) and ② Ab lydian mode (Ab, Bb, C, D, Eb, F, G with pedals: A♭, B♭, C♮, D♮, E♭, F♮, G♮) are standard diatonic glissandi.  \n③ a minor harmonic's DVII7 chord (interpreted as G#dim7: G#, B, D, F) and ④ G major's D7 chord (D, F#, A, C) are achievable via specialized pedal settings that create enharmonic equivalents, repeating the four chord tones across the seven strings per octave; ⑤ chromatic scale cannot, as it requires all 12 pitch classes, exceeding the diatonic framework.",
