    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A and D are consonant (altered to m6 and M3, respectively) and altered (chromatic accidentals modifying the generic spans).\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\nThe reasoning identifies consonant intervals as those measuring 0, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, or 9 semitones (P1, m/M3, P4, P5, m/M6, P8), while altered intervals feature accidentals that modify the generic diatonic span. For each option, compute the semitone distance by starting with the natural generic interval based on letter span and adjusting for accidentals: raising the lower note or upper note increases the size, lowering decreases it.\n\n- A (\"♯c - ♯♯F\"): Generic C-F is P4 (5 semitones); ♯c (+1) and ♯♯F (+2) add 3 semitones, yielding 8 semitones (m6, consonant) and altered.\n- B (\"♭b - ♯b\"): Generic B-B is P1 (0 semitones); ♭b (-1 on lower, +1 size) and ♯b (+1 on upper, +1 size) add 2 semitones, yielding 2 semitones (major second, dissonant).\n- C (\"♭♭A - ♭♭G\"): Generic G-A (adjacent letters) is major second (2 semitones); ♭♭G (-2 on lower, +2 size) and ♭♭A (-2 on upper, -2 size) net 0 change, yielding 2 semitones (dissonant).\n- D (\"♯♯D - C\"): Generic C-D (adjacent letters) is major second (2 semitones); ♯♯D (+2 on lower, +2 size) and C (0) add 2 semitones, yielding 4 semitones (M3, consonant) and altered.\n\nThus, A and D are both consonant and altered, corresponding to option D (AD).",
