      "generated_answer": "♭E (Eb), ♭G (F♯ as Gong in Yu mode), and G (Jue in pentatonic/Gong mode) all imply tonic Eb via standard pentatonic interval structures (4, 3, and 0 semitones offset, respectively).  \nYan-yue Zhi mode relative pitches (0, 2, 5, 7, 9 semitones from tonic) lack a note 3 semitones above tonic, so ♭G (F♯ as Run) cannot imply tonic Eb.  \nThus, C does not share the tonic.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer identifies C as the option not sharing the same tonic pitch, Eb, based on standard Chinese pentatonic mode structures and semitone intervals. The pentatonic scale follows intervals of 2-2-3-2 semitones, with modes as rotations: Gong mode (0-2-4-7-9 semitones from tonic), Shang mode (0-2-5-7-10), Zhi mode (0-2-5-7-9), and Yu mode (0-3-5-7-10).  \n\nFor A (♭E Ya-yue Shang mode), ♭E (Eb, semitone 3) is the tonic of Shang mode (offset 0), confirming tonic Eb. For B (Qing-yue Yu mode, ♭G = F♯ at semitone 6 as Gong), Gong is +3 semitones from Yu tonic, so tonic = 6 - 3 = 3 (Eb). For D (pentatonic/Gong mode, G at semitone 7 as Jue), Jue is +4 semitones from Gong tonic, so tonic = 7 - 4 = 3 (Eb).  \n\nFor C (Yan-yue Zhi mode, ♭G = F♯ at semitone 6 as Run note), Zhi mode pitches are 0-2-5-7-9 semitones from tonic; no position matches +3 semitones (required for 6 - 3 = 3), as 3 is absent, making tonic Eb impossible.",
