      "generated_answer": "**Sinfonia Antarctica** (Ralph Vaughan Williams' Symphony No. 7)\n\nThis matches Vaughan Williams' Sinfonia Antarctica perfectly: its finale (Epilogue) opens with a soprano's wordless pentatonic melody over a drum roll, and the second-movement (Romanza) trumpet cadenza was inspired by the composer hearing a New Zealand Army bugler play a natural harmonic seventh while sounding \"The Last Post.\" It is the standard identification for this description.\n\n\\boxed{Sinfonia Antarctica}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the key descriptors: a finale opening with a soprano soloist's wordless pentatonic melody over a drumroll, and a second-movement trumpet cadenza inspired by a bugler playing a harmonic seventh. These elements point to Vaughan Williams' Symphony No. 7, known by the nickname Sinfonia Antarctica, as it features exactly these traits—the Epilogue (finale) starts with the specified soprano line over a drumroll, while the Romanza (second movement) includes a trumpet cadenza derived from the composer's observation of a bugler producing a natural harmonic seventh during \"The Last Post.\" Cross-referencing eliminates other candidates, such as Mahler's symphonies (which have vocal elements but not wordless pentatonic over drumroll) or Dvořák's New World Symphony (pentatonic but no soprano), confirming Sinfonia Antarctica as the precise and most common match.",
