      "generated_answer": "D\n\nThe most obvious Inca war motivations were territorial expansion (B) and resource acquisition (E), making D the less obvious reason, as Inca wars served to test and promote capable leaders through battlefield performance. This aligns with historical accounts of their merit-based military hierarchy. Options A and C are more conventional strategic aims.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying obvious Inca war motivations, such as territorial expansion (option B) and resource control (option E), which are excluded as they do not qualify as \"less obvious.\" It then evaluates the remaining options: A (demonstrating prowess) and C (uniting the population) as relatively conventional strategies, while D (identifying and cultivating leaders) stands out due to historical Inca practices where warfare provided a merit-based system for selecting and training elites through battlefield performance. Thus, D is selected as the most likely correct answer based on its unique alignment with less obvious socio-political functions of Inca conflicts.",
