    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4503",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"At least one of the [world's] societies would have to somehow enormously increase its productivity [in order to achieve global hegemony]. That quantum jump would have to be made before the various scientific, technological, agricultural, and industrial revolutions on which our post-quantum-leap world rests. It could only be accomplished by exploiting the ecosystems, mineral resources, and human assets of whole continents outside the lands of the society making the jump. Western Europe did just that by means of its brutality and guns and, more important, by geographical and ecological luck.\"\nCopyright © 2015 Cambridge University Press.\nAlfred Crosby, historian, Ecological Imperialism, 2004\nBased on your knowledge of world history, which of the following contributed LEAST to Western European global hegemony between 1450 and 1750 C.E.?\n\nOptions: \nA. The implementation of joint-stock companies\nB. The exploitation of labor in colonized regions\nC. The exploration of new sea routes to Asia and the Americas\nD. The establishment of trading posts along Asian and African coasts\nE. The exchange of food sources between the Americas and Europe\nF. The spread of Christianity to non-European regions\nG. The development and application of steam-powered technologies\nH. The discovery and colonization of the Americas\nI. The development of advanced naval technology\nJ. Refinement of gunpowder technologies\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**G**\n\nSteam-powered technologies were not developed or widely applied until after 1750 (e.g., Watt's improvements in the 1760s-1780s), making them irrelevant to European hegemony in the 1450-1750 period. All other options directly fueled exploration, trade, conquest, and economic gains during this era. The quote emphasizes pre-industrial exploitation, aligning with this.\n\n\\boxed{G}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by identifying the question's focus: determining which factor contributed least to Western European global hegemony from 1450 to 1750 C.E., a period defined by exploration, colonization, and mercantilism, as highlighted in the provided quote from Alfred Crosby. Each option is evaluated against historical events of that era: A (joint-stock companies) financed ventures like the Dutch VOC; B (labor exploitation) drove economies via slavery and encomienda systems; C (new sea routes) enabled direct trade through figures like Columbus and da Gama; D (trading posts) established Portuguese dominance; E (Columbian Exchange) boosted European agriculture and population; F (spread of Christianity) justified conquests; H (Americas' discovery) provided vast resources; I (naval tech) supported transoceanic voyages; and J (gunpowder refinements) facilitated military superiority. Option G (steam-powered technologies) is excluded because its key developments, such as Newcomen's engine (1712) and Watt's improvements (post-1760s), occurred after or at the very end of the period and did not contribute to hegemony until the Industrial Revolution. Thus, G is the least relevant based on temporal and historical misalignment with the era's drivers of dominance.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 337,
        "output_tokens": 340,
        "total_tokens": 1188
      },
      "provider": "azure",
