    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4598",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nRead the the following poem to answer questions.\nTake up the White Man's burden—\nSend forth the best ye breed—\nGo bind your sons to exile\nTo serve your captives' need;\nTo wait in heavy harness,\nOn fluttered folk and wild—\nYour new-caught, sullen peoples,\nHalf-devil and half-child.\n...\nTake up the White Man's burden—\nThe savage wars of peace—\nFill full the mouth of Famine\nAnd bid the sickness cease;\nAnd when your goal is nearest\nThe end for others sought…\nWatch sloth and heathen Folly\nBring all your hopes to naught.\nRudyard Kipling, The White Man's Burden, 1899\nIt is generally acknowledged that Kipling fashioned White Man's Burden to address the subject of the American colonization of the Philippines, which the United States had recently won from Spain in the Spanish-American War. With that information in mind, what message can Kipling be said to be offering the Americans in this excerpt?\n\nOptions: \nA. A call for all Western nations to unite in the effort of colonization\nB. A critique of the American government for engaging in the act of colonization\nC. An appeal to respect the cultural differences and practices of colonized peoples\nD. An exhortation to Christianize the heathen peoples of the world\nE. A warning to avoid the war and hardships of imperialism\nF. An endorsement of the use of force and military might in colonization\nG. A plea to the Americans to return the colonized peoples to their homelands\nH. An encouragement to Americans to seize the economic opportunities offered by the colonies\nI. A reminder of the responsibility of advanced civilizations to bring the benefits of modern civilization to less-developed peoples\nJ. An ironic presentation of the sheer folly of imperialism\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I. A reminder of the responsibility of advanced civilizations to bring the benefits of modern civilization to less-developed peoples\n\nThe poem urges Americans (\"ye breed\") to sacrifice and serve \"half-devil and half-child\" peoples by ending famine, sickness, and folly, framing imperialism as a noble duty despite ingratitude. This aligns directly with option I, contrasting critiques (B, J) or rejections (E, G). Other options mismatch the exhortative tone and paternalistic view of colonized peoples.\n\n\\boxed{I}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by analyzing the poem's context and content: Kipling addresses Americans post-Spanish-American War, urging them to \"Take up the White Man's burden\" by sending their best to serve \"new-caught, sullen peoples, Half-devil and half-child,\" ending famine and sickness despite challenges like \"sloth and heathen Folly.\" This portrays imperialism as a sacrificial duty of advanced civilizations to uplift less-developed ones. Evaluating options, A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, and J are ruled out for not matching the exhortative, paternalistic tone—A seeks unity, B and J critique, C respects differences, D focuses narrowly on Christianization, E warns against, F emphasizes force, G suggests return, H stresses economics—leaving I as the precise fit for the responsibility to bring modern benefits.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 417,
        "output_tokens": 278,
        "total_tokens": 1218
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.000315305,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
