    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4625",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"It's come! It's posted at the district mayor's office,\" a passerby shouted at me as he ran. I reached the Rue Drout in one leap. … I read the message at a glance. … \"The First Day of Mobilization Will Be Sunday, August 2 [1914].\" … It was an announcement to a million and a half Frenchmen. … War! … Dead tired but exhilarated, I got back to [my newspaper's office] and burst into the office of Georges Clemenceau, our chief. \"What is Paris saying?\" he asked me. \"It's singing, sir!\" \"It will be all right then[,\" Clemenceau replied].\nRoland Doregelès, After 50 Years, c. 1965\nFrom the passage, one may infer that Clemenceau\n\nOptions: \nA. had already started making strategies for the upcoming war\nB. was in disagreement with the general public's reaction to the war\nC. was unaware of the announcement of war until informed by the narrator\nD. had grave doubts about France's ability to win a war against Germany\nE. was fearful of the upcoming war and its potential consequences\nF. was relieved to hear that Paris was reacting with joy at the advent of war\nG. would one day serve as prime minister of France\nH. believed that the war would bring economic prosperity to France\nI. was indifferent to the reaction of Paris towards the war\nJ. was disgusted to hear that Paris was reacting with joy at the advent of war\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**F**\n\nClemenceau's response, \"It will be all right then,\" directly follows the narrator's report that Paris is \"singing\" (joyful about the war), indicating he views the public's enthusiasm positively as a reassuring sign of national resolve. This aligns with relief at the joyful reaction, ruling out disagreement (B), disgust (J), indifference (I), fear (E), or doubt (D). Other options like prior strategies (A), unawareness (C), future role (G), or economic beliefs (H) lack textual support.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 376,
        "output_tokens": 118,
        "total_tokens": 1152
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0001342,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4626",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n[T]he the end and measure of this power, when in every man's hands in the state of nature, being the preservation of all of his society, that is, all mankind in general, it can have no other end or measure, when in the hands of the magistrate, but to preserve the members of that society in their lives, liberties, and possessions, and so cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over their lives and fortunes, which are as much as possible to be preserved, but a power to make law, and annex such penalties to them, as may tend to the preservation of the whole by cutting off those parts, and those only, which are so corrupt that they threaten the sound and healthy, without which no severity is lawful. And this power has its original only from compact, and agreement, and the mutual consent of those who make up the community. …\nWhensoever, therefore, the legislative shall transgress this fundamental rule of society; and either by ambition, fear, folly or corruption, endeavor to grasp themselves, or put into the hands of any other, an absolute power over the lives, liberties, and estates of the people; by this breach of trust they forfeit the power the people had put into their hands for quite contrary ends.\nJohn Locke, Second Treatise of Government, 1690\nFrom the passage, one may infer that Locke believed a government loses its legitimacy when\n\nOptions: \nA. when the people wish to change governors\nB. it does not have penalties for breaking the law\nC. it tries to exercise absolute power\nD. it does not have a legislative body\nE. it does not enact laws\nF. it is weak and can be overthrown\nG. the people do not follow the laws\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the passage, Locke explicitly states that the legislative \"transgress this fundamental rule of society\" and \"forfeit the power the people had put into their hands\" when they \"endeavor to grasp themselves, or put into the hands of any other, an absolute power over the lives, liberties, and estates of the people.\" This directly indicates that a government loses legitimacy when it attempts to exercise absolute, arbitrary power rather than being limited by its compact with the people. The passage emphasizes that governmental power \"cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power.\"\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 460,
        "output_tokens": 126,
        "total_tokens": 586
      },
      "provider": "anthropic/bedrock"
    },
    "cost": 0.0032700000000000003,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
