    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4632",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nIs it not unity alone that can weld us into an effective force, capable of creating our own progress and making our valuable contribution to world peace? Which independent African state will claim that its financial structure and banking institutions are fully harnessed to its national development? Which will claim that its material resources and human energies are available for its own national aspirations? We are fast learning that political independence is not enough to rid us of the consequences of colonial rule. We have been too busy nursing our separate states to understand fully the basic need for union, rooted in common purpose, common planning and common endeavour.\nGhana's president, Kwame Nkrumah, addressing the Organization of African Unity, 1963\nWhich of the following factors was most important in preventing the fulfillment of the political dreams described in the passage?\n\nOptions: \nA. A lack of common ethnicity, language, or religion\nB. The spread of communism throughout the continent\nC. Armed intervention on the part of the Cold War superpowers\nD. Africa's shortage of natural resources\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "C. Armed intervention on the part of the Cold War superpowers\n\nThe passage advocates for pan-African political and economic unity beyond independence, but Cold War superpowers (US and USSR) intervened through proxy conflicts, coups, and support for rival factions (e.g., Congo Crisis, Angola), fostering divisions that undermined such unity. While ethnic diversity (A) posed challenges, Nkrumah emphasized overcoming it via common purpose; communism's spread (B) was limited and not a primary barrier; and Africa faced no general resource shortage (D).  \n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
