    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4681",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"I observe the great and wonderful mistake, both our own and our fathers, as to the civil powers of this world, acting in spiritual matters. I have read…the last will and testament of the Lord Jesus over many times, and yet I cannot find by one tittle of that testament that if He had been pleased to have accepted of a temporal crown and government that ever He would have put forth the least finger of temporal or civil power in the matters of His spiritual affairs and Kingdom. Hence must it lamentably be against the testimony of Christ Jesus for the civil state to impose upon the souls of the people a religion, a worship, a ministry, oaths (in religious and civil affairs), tithes, times, days, marryings, and buryings in holy ground…\"\nRoger Williams, The Hireling Ministry None of Christ's, 1652\nThe \"First Great Awakening\" can be seen as a direct response to which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. Existentialism\nB. The Civil War\nC. The Reformation\nD. The Industrial Revolution\nE. The Age of Exploration\nF. Transcendentalism\nG. Puritanism\nH. Romanticism\nI. The French Revolution\nJ. The Enlightenment\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The First Great Awakening was a religious revival movement in the American colonies during the 1730s-1740s that emphasized personal religious experience and emotional connection to faith. It emerged as a response to the rationalism and emphasis on reason that characterized the Enlightenment period, which had led to declining religious fervor and more intellectual approaches to religion. The revival sought to counter Enlightenment rationalism by reinvigorating emotional and experiential aspects of Christian faith.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 351,
        "output_tokens": 108,
        "total_tokens": 459
      },
      "provider": "anthropic/bedrock"
    },
    "cost": 0.002673,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4682",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"In one view the slaveholders have a decided advantage over all opposition. It is well to notice this advantage—the advantage of complete organization. They are organized; and yet were not at the pains of creating their organizations. The State governments, where the system of slavery exists, are complete slavery organizations. The church organizations in those States are equally at the service of slavery; while the Federal Government, with its army and navy, from the chief magistracy in Washington, to the Supreme Court, and thence to the chief marshalship at New York, is pledged to support, defend, and propagate the crying curse of human bondage. The pen, the purse, and the sword, are united against the simple truth, preached by humble men in obscure places.\"\nFrederick Douglass, 1857\nIn his opinion on the case Dred Scott v. Sandford, Chief Justice Roger Taney upheld the sentiment above by stating that\n\nOptions: \nA. corporations were entitled to the same protections guaranteed to individuals under the Fourteenth Amendment\nB. the Fugitive Slave Act was unconstitutional\nC. women had the right to vote under the United States Constitution\nD. separate but equal facilities for people of different races was constitutional\nE. every state had the right to secede from the Union\nF. the abolitionist movement was a threat to the United States Constitution\nG. school prayer violated the principle of \"separation of church and state\"\nH. the Second Amendment allowed individuals to keep and bear arms\nI. slavery was a state's rights issue and should be decided by popular sovereignty\nJ. Congress had no right to regulate slavery in United States territories\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**J**\n\nIn *Dred Scott v. Sandford* (1857), Chief Justice Taney ruled that Congress lacked authority to ban slavery in federal territories, invalidating the Missouri Compromise and reinforcing the pro-slavery structures Douglass criticized. This decision upheld slaveholders' \"advantage of complete organization\" by limiting federal interference. Other options refer to unrelated cases or issues.\n\n\\boxed{J}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer, J, is derived from analyzing Frederick Douglass's 1857 quote, which highlights systemic support for slavery across state, church, and federal institutions, and linking it to Chief Justice Roger Taney's opinion in *Dred Scott v. Sandford*. Taney's ruling declared that Congress had no authority to regulate or prohibit slavery in U.S. territories, directly aligning with the quote's depiction of federal backing for slavery by striking down the Missouri Compromise. Among the options, J precisely matches this aspect of the decision, while others pertain to unrelated historical contexts, such as later amendments (A), different cases (D), or unrelated rights (C, E, G, H).",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 391,
        "output_tokens": 220,
        "total_tokens": 987
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0003426,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
