    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4709",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nOur leaders talk about stopping aggression from the north, but this was a struggle among groups of Vietnamese until we intervened. We seem bent upon saving the Vietnamese from Ho Chi Minh even if we have to kill them and demolish their country to do it. As the native people survey bombed-out villages, women and children burned by napalm, rice crops destroyed and cities overrun with our military personnel, they are doubtless saying secretly of the Vietcong guerillas and of the American forces, \"A plague on both your houses.\" … Stop the bombing, north and south, end search and destroy offensive sweeps, and confine our military action to holding operations on the ground. Bombing the north has failed to halt or seriously check the flow of troops to the south and may, in fact, have prompted a much greater war effort by Hanoi.\n—Senator George McGovern, \"The Lessons of Vietnam,\" April 25, 1967\nThe sentiments expressed in the speech above most directly influenced which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. The resignation of Richard Nixon in 1974\nB. The decision of the U.S. to engage in the Korean War\nC. The escalation of the Cold War in the 1970s\nD. The Tet Offensive of 1968\nE. The signing of the Paris Peace Accords in 1973\nF. The formation of the Vietnam Veterans Against the War in 1967\nG. The emergence of a youth counterculture\nH. The passage of the War Powers Act of 1973\nI. The start of the Vietnam War in 1955\nJ. The initiation of the Civil Rights Movement in the 1960s\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**H**\n\nMcGovern's 1967 speech expressed strong anti-war sentiments criticizing U.S. escalation and calling for de-escalation, reflecting growing congressional opposition to executive war powers. These views contributed directly to legislative reforms like the War Powers Act of 1973, passed to require congressional approval for prolonged military actions and prevent future Vietnam-style interventions. Other options, such as the Paris Accords or VVAW formation, were influenced by broader anti-war pressures but not as directly tied to this congressional critique.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 402,
        "output_tokens": 106,
        "total_tokens": 2789
      },
      "provider": "azure",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00025865,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4710",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The seeds of totalitarian regimes are nurtured by misery and want. They spread and grow in the evil soil of poverty and strife. They reach their full growth when the hope of a people for a better life has died. We must keep that hope alive. . . . Great responsibilities have been placed upon us by the swift movement of events. . . . I am confident that the Congress will face these responsibilities squarely.\"\n—President Harry S. Truman, 1947\nThe passage above can best be seen as providing a rationale for\n\nOptions: \nA. the policy of containment.\nB. the start of the Cold War.\nC. the establishment of the United Nations.\nD. participation in the Atlantic Charter.\nE. the creation of NATO.\nF. embarking on a \"roll-back\" of communism.\nG. the principle of \"massive retaliation.\"\nH. the policy of détente.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage from Truman's 1947 address to Congress justifies aiding nations to prevent the spread of totalitarian regimes (i.e., communism) by addressing poverty and despair, aligning directly with the Truman Doctrine and the policy of containment.  \nOther options are mismatched chronologically or thematically (e.g., NATO in 1949, détente in the 1970s).  \n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 240,
        "output_tokens": 77,
        "total_tokens": 699
