    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4718",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The greatest contribution that Africa can make to the peace of the world is to avoid all the dangers inherent in disunity, by creating a political union which will also by its success, stand as an example to a divided world. A Union of African states will project more effectively the African personality. It will command respect from a world that has regard only for size and influence. The scant attention paid to African opposition to the French atomic tests in the Sahara, and the ignominious spectacle of the U.N. in the Congo quibbling about constitutional niceties while the Republic was tottering into anarchy, are evidence of the callous disregard of African Independence by the Great Powers.\"\nKwame Nkrumah, Ghanian leader, 1961\nWhich of the following explains why Nkrumah feels Africa must \"avoid all the dangers inherent in disunity\"?\n\nOptions: \nA. Disunity might lead to the failure of political unions within the African continent.\nB. African nations will not achieve independence without unity.\nC. Disunity is what originally led many African nations into disarray.\nD. The world community sees power as a function of size and influence.\nE. The African continent has already experienced its share of civil wars.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nNkrumah argues that disunity leaves Africa weak and ignored by great powers, as shown by events like the French atomic tests and the UN's response in Congo. He emphasizes that a united Africa would gain respect because \"a world that has regard only for size and influence\" values collective power. Option D directly captures this reasoning from the quote.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe provided answer, D, is derived by analyzing Nkrumah's quote, which links Africa's disunity to its lack of global respect and influence. The text states that a \"Union of African states will... command respect from a world that has regard only for size and influence,\" and cites examples like the French atomic tests and the UN in Congo as evidence of disregard due to fragmentation. Among the options, D aligns precisely with this by stating \"The world community sees power as a function of size and influence,\" while A is circular, B misaligns with the post-independence context, C and E introduce unmentioned historical causes.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 309,
        "output_tokens": 206,
        "total_tokens": 897
