    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4724",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What do anthropologists NOT need to know to determine when people entered the Americas from Siberia?\n\nOptions: \nA. When sled dogs were first domesticated\nB. When eastern Siberia was first inhabited\nC. When the Bering Strait was exposed and open for travel\nD. The timeline of the Ice Age\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**A**\n\nTo determine the timing of human migration from Siberia to the Americas via Beringia, anthropologists require knowledge of Siberian habitation (B), Bering Strait exposure (C), and Ice Age timelines (D) for feasible travel conditions. Sled dog domestication (A) is irrelevant, as it occurred millennia later and was not involved in the Ice Age pedestrian crossings.  \n\\boxed{A}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates each option's relevance to the Beringia migration theory during the Ice Age. Options B, C, and D are essential: B establishes human presence in eastern Siberia, C identifies the land bridge's availability due to lower sea levels, and D provides the glacial context for migration windows around 15,000–20,000 years ago. Option A is unnecessary, as sled dog domestication occurred much later (roughly 2,000–5,000 years ago) and played no role in the initial foot-based crossings. Thus, A is the factor anthropologists do not need.",
