      "generated_answer": "The excerpt from John C. Calhoun's \"South Carolina Exposition and Protest\" was written in response to the Tariff of 1828, also known as the \"Tariff of Abominations,\" which imposed high duties on imported goods. Calhoun argued that states had the right to nullify federal laws they deemed unconstitutional, particularly this tariff, which he believed harmed Southern economic interests. The passage centers on states' rights to judge constitutional violations, directly tied to opposition against increased tariff rates.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
