    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4750",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nOh, shame on you, poor Winter King!\nWhat's this that you have done?\nIs 't not a very naughty thing\nTo snatch the kaiser's crown?\nNow you will have to stay away\nAlike from Rhine and Prague,\nAnd more than that-shame and dismay\nYour days and nights will plague.\nRight well you knew, and all the world,\nRight well they know this thing,\nThat Ferdinand alone can be\nBohemia's lawful king.\nSo come, dear Fritz, rouse up and go\nTo Ferdinand, your king,\nAnd beg him graciously to show\nFull pardon for your sin.\n\"Poor Winter King,\" seventeenth-century song\nWhich of the following is an important underlying issue in this song?\n\nOptions: \nA. Political corruption\nB. Civil disobedience\nC. Rules of royal succession\nD. Gender inequality\nE. Social class conflicts\nF. Diplomatic relations between nations\nG. Environmental concerns\nH. Religious conflict\nI. Economic instability\nJ. Military stalemate\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe song emphasizes Ferdinand as Bohemia's \"lawful king\" and condemns the Winter King (Frederick V) for unlawfully \"snatch[ing] the kaiser's crown,\" highlighting disputes over legitimate royal authority and succession. While religious tensions fueled the historical context (Thirty Years' War), the lyrics focus explicitly on political legitimacy rather than faith. Other options like corruption or diplomacy lack direct support in the text.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by analyzing the song's content, which criticizes the Winter King for usurping the Kaiser's crown and declares Ferdinand the \"lawful king\" of Bohemia, directly pointing to a dispute over legitimate rule. This aligns most closely with option C, Rules of royal succession, as the lyrics stress rightful kingship and urge submission to Ferdinand. Other options are evaluated and dismissed: religious conflict (H) is historically relevant but not mentioned; political corruption (A), civil disobedience (B), and others like gender inequality (D) or economic instability (I) have no textual basis, confirming C as the primary underlying issue.",
      "success": true,
