    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4803",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The skulls of the earliest migrants to the New World do not match those of modern Native Americans. This suggests that:\n\nOptions: \nA. the early migrants were not human.\nB. the early migrants did not contribute to the genetic pool of modern Native Americans.\nC. the early migrants were a separate species.\nD. craniometrics is an unreliable method of tracking populations.\nE. skull shapes can change dramatically over generations.\nF. modern Native Americans have very different DNA.\nG. the findings are not significant and can be disregarded.\nH. the \"skull\" haplogroup was not passed on.\nI. modern Native Americans underwent a significant evolution.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**E**\n\nSkull morphology can evolve rapidly due to genetic drift, natural selection, diet, or admixture, explaining mismatches between ancient and modern populations without implying discontinuity. Genetic evidence confirms continuity from early migrants to modern Native Americans. Options like B or I overstate discontinuity or imply major separate evolution unsupported by DNA data.\n\n\\boxed{E}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process begins by analyzing the question: the mismatch between skulls of earliest New World migrants and modern Native Americans requires an explanation grounded in scientific principles of population genetics and morphology. Options are evaluated systematically: A and C are dismissed as they absurdly suggest non-human or separate species status, contradicted by archaeological evidence; B and F are rejected due to genetic studies showing continuity; D questions the method without direct support; G ignores evidence; H misapplies genetic terminology; I implies overly broad evolutionary changes. This leaves E as the most precise, as cranial features demonstrably evolve over generations via mechanisms like genetic drift, selection, and admixture, directly accounting for the observed discrepancy while aligning with established paleoanthropological consensus.",
      "success": true,
